1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,832
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl. Today is October 6.

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:05,333
Seven stories today:

3
00:00:05,353 --> 00:00:08,037
ChatGPT tests ads during image generation,

4
00:00:08,057 --> 00:00:10,417
OpenAI watermarks text in the EU,

5
00:00:10,437 --> 00:00:13,037
Reflection unveils the open model Beam,

6
00:00:13,057 --> 00:00:15,817
Wikipedia finds agents it believes are OpenAI's,

7
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:17,997
Reka puts text, images and video in one model,

8
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:19,737
Cohere launches North 2,

9
00:00:19,757 --> 00:00:21,862
and an open model for finding bugs,

10
00:00:21,882 --> 00:00:23,513
apex-flash-1.

11
00:00:23,533 --> 00:00:26,347
First, when you generate images in ChatGPT,

12
00:00:26,367 --> 00:00:28,448
ads may start showing up next to them.

13
00:00:28,468 --> 00:00:30,079
On October 5, OpenAI announced that

14
00:00:30,099 --> 00:00:31,249
in image generation,

15
00:00:31,269 --> 00:00:33,338
it will test a new visual ad format.

16
00:00:33,358 --> 00:00:35,982
Testing starts later this month in the US,

17
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:37,818
with an initial group of advertisers.

18
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:38,586
OpenAI says

19
00:00:38,606 --> 00:00:41,575
ChatGPT reaches 1.2 billion users every week.

20
00:00:41,595 --> 00:00:42,699
Ads will be clearly labeled

21
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:44,625
and kept separate from your image.

22
00:00:44,645 --> 00:00:48,025
OpenAI says ads don't affect ChatGPT's answers.

23
00:00:48,045 --> 00:00:48,562
OpenAI also

24
00:00:48,582 --> 00:00:51,245
expanded measurement tools for advertisers,

25
00:00:51,265 --> 00:00:52,701
adding partners such as AppsFlyer,

26
00:00:52,721 --> 00:00:54,595
Adjust and more.

27
00:00:54,615 --> 00:00:56,782
It's also working with DoubleVerify and IAS

28
00:00:56,802 --> 00:00:58,366
on brand suitability pilots,

29
00:00:58,386 --> 00:01:01,606
so advertisers can see what surrounds their ads.

30
00:01:01,626 --> 00:01:03,961
TechCrunch says that once ads roll out globally,

31
00:01:03,981 --> 00:01:06,850
advertisers can reach those 1.2 billion users.

32
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:07,735
TechCrunch notes

33
00:01:07,755 --> 00:01:09,246
the move follows Meta's launch

34
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:11,480
of its free agent app, Muse.

35
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,786
Someone always pays for the free paintbrush.

36
00:01:13,986 --> 00:01:15,026
Second, in the EU,

37
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:16,821
text written with ChatGPT

38
00:01:16,841 --> 00:01:19,295
will soon carry an invisible watermark.

39
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:21,064
On October 5, OpenAI said

40
00:01:21,084 --> 00:01:22,846
that to comply with the EU AI Act,

41
00:01:22,866 --> 00:01:24,305
over the coming weeks, in the EU,

42
00:01:24,325 --> 00:01:27,342
it will watermark text output from ChatGPT and Codex.

43
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:30,187
How? The technique is called textGrain.

44
00:01:30,207 --> 00:01:33,504
It hides a statistical signal in the model's word choices.

45
00:01:33,524 --> 00:01:36,334
API customers worldwide can opt in.

46
00:01:36,354 --> 00:01:37,900
It's off by default.

47
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:39,868
In OpenAI's tests on psychology text,

48
00:01:39,888 --> 00:01:41,351
at a 1% false positive rate,

49
00:01:41,371 --> 00:01:43,063
400-token passages

50
00:01:43,083 --> 00:01:45,064
were detected about 95% of the time.

51
00:01:45,084 --> 00:01:48,391
But rewriting weakens it: replace 25% of the words,

52
00:01:48,411 --> 00:01:50,642
and detection drops to 17%.

53
00:01:50,662 --> 00:01:52,646
Math text is harder:

54
00:01:52,666 --> 00:01:55,467
at 400 tokens, only about 60% is detected.

55
00:01:55,487 --> 00:01:56,566
At first, the detector

56
00:01:56,586 --> 00:01:59,484
will only go to approved researchers and expert organizations.

57
00:01:59,504 --> 00:02:01,409
The Decoder points out that

58
00:02:01,429 --> 00:02:02,737
Anthropic, for Claude,

59
00:02:02,757 --> 00:02:04,209
uses a watermark that is mandatory worldwide,

60
00:02:04,229 --> 00:02:06,798
while OpenAI's API version is optional.

61
00:02:06,818 --> 00:02:08,518
A watermark can catch copy and paste,

62
00:02:08,538 --> 00:02:10,292
but not someone who edits.

63
00:02:10,492 --> 00:02:13,747
Third, US startup Reflection AI

64
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:16,947
unveiled its first open-weight model, Beam,

65
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:18,724
with 501B total parameters.

66
00:02:18,744 --> 00:02:20,291
Released on October 5,

67
00:02:20,311 --> 00:02:21,605
it's a mixture of experts

68
00:02:21,625 --> 00:02:23,379
that activates only 23B parameters at a time.

69
00:02:23,399 --> 00:02:25,411
Reflection says that on hard reasoning tests,

70
00:02:25,431 --> 00:02:27,540
it scores comparably to GLM-5.2

71
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:30,509
while using three to four times less inference compute.

72
00:02:30,529 --> 00:02:32,021
On coding and agent tasks,

73
00:02:32,041 --> 00:02:34,286
it approaches Alibaba's Qwen 3.8-Max.

74
00:02:34,306 --> 00:02:36,126
It was pretrained on 23.8T tokens,

75
00:02:36,146 --> 00:02:39,019
and reinforcement learning ran on 10.5K GB300 GPUs

76
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:40,378
for four weeks.

77
00:02:40,398 --> 00:02:42,288
The license is Apache 2.0.

78
00:02:42,308 --> 00:02:43,808
But the weights aren't out yet.

79
00:02:43,828 --> 00:02:46,085
Reflection says they're coming later this month.

80
00:02:46,105 --> 00:02:47,189
Semafor notes

81
00:02:47,209 --> 00:02:49,152
that Qwen 3.8-Max itself still trails

82
00:02:49,172 --> 00:02:51,431
OpenAI's and Anthropic's top

83
00:02:51,451 --> 00:02:52,535
closed models.

84
00:02:52,555 --> 00:02:54,230
Semafor also reports

85
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:55,933
Reflection is backed by

86
00:02:55,953 --> 00:02:57,570
Nvidia, Sequoia and Citigroup,

87
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:01,066
and its June round valued it at $25 billion pre-money.

88
00:03:01,086 --> 00:03:02,335
The open door is there,

89
00:03:02,355 --> 00:03:04,355
but the key is still a few weeks away.

90
00:03:04,555 --> 00:03:05,183
Fourth,

91
00:03:05,203 --> 00:03:07,775
the Wikimedia Foundation, which runs Wikipedia,

92
00:03:07,795 --> 00:03:08,813
says it found

93
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:11,335
agents it believes are operated by OpenAI.

94
00:03:11,355 --> 00:03:12,625
Its October 5 blog post says

95
00:03:12,645 --> 00:03:13,520
the agents

96
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,385
made millions of automated requests to public APIs

97
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:17,925
and crawled millions of pages,

98
00:03:17,945 --> 00:03:20,300
mainly from Wikidata and Commons.

99
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:22,065
They also made test edits in sandboxes

100
00:03:22,085 --> 00:03:24,085
and changed a citation tool's configuration,

101
00:03:24,105 --> 00:03:26,455
which the foundation believes was meant to use it as a proxy

102
00:03:26,475 --> 00:03:28,088
for fetching other sites.

103
00:03:28,108 --> 00:03:29,177
There were also a few attempts to compromise

104
00:03:29,197 --> 00:03:31,586
the public Etherpad, all unsuccessful.

105
00:03:31,606 --> 00:03:33,232
Hundreds of thousands of data queries

106
00:03:33,252 --> 00:03:35,413
also hit the Wikidata Query Service.

107
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:36,621
The foundation says this traffic

108
00:03:36,641 --> 00:03:37,568
may have contributed, in May,

109
00:03:37,588 --> 00:03:40,697
to a partial outage of the Wikidata Query Service.

110
00:03:40,717 --> 00:03:42,719
The good news: it found no evidence that its systems

111
00:03:42,739 --> 00:03:44,384
or data were compromised.

112
00:03:44,404 --> 00:03:46,051
Note: "believed to be OpenAI's"

113
00:03:46,071 --> 00:03:47,829
is the foundation's own assessment.

114
00:03:47,849 --> 00:03:51,542
Agents that go out to work need to learn to knock first.

115
00:03:51,742 --> 00:03:54,132
Fifth, a single 19B model

116
00:03:54,152 --> 00:03:55,715
that handles text, images, video,

117
00:03:55,735 --> 00:03:57,548
and robot actions.

118
00:03:57,568 --> 00:04:00,178
Reka AI released Rho-1 on October 5.

119
00:04:00,198 --> 00:04:01,962
It turns all of these into tokens

120
00:04:01,982 --> 00:04:03,615
inside one network.

121
00:04:03,635 --> 00:04:05,980
The Decoder says it uses no tool calls

122
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,447
or external models.

123
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:09,626
Training used just 320 H100 GPUs

124
00:04:09,646 --> 00:04:10,962
for about three months.

125
00:04:10,982 --> 00:04:13,240
In Reka's internal tests, its distilled version

126
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,346
returned a 5.3-second video clip in about a second.

127
00:04:16,366 --> 00:04:17,925
Where does robot data come from?

128
00:04:17,945 --> 00:04:19,728
An inverse dynamics model

129
00:04:19,748 --> 00:04:22,321
pulls actions out of ordinary web videos.

130
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:24,182
It's only a research preview,

131
00:04:24,202 --> 00:04:25,734
and Reka hasn't said anything about open weights.

132
00:04:25,754 --> 00:04:28,469
Video is currently capped at 672×384,

133
00:04:28,489 --> 00:04:29,896
and Reka admits

134
00:04:29,916 --> 00:04:33,477
consistency across varied prompts is still brittle.

135
00:04:33,497 --> 00:04:35,563
A small model wants to do it all.

136
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:37,385
Real-world tests will tell.

137
00:04:37,585 --> 00:04:39,662
Sixth, companies using AI agents

138
00:04:39,682 --> 00:04:40,760
fear two things:

139
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:43,617
runaway bills, and starting from scratch every time.

140
00:04:43,637 --> 00:04:45,330
On October 5, Cohere released

141
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:47,148
its enterprise agent platform, North 2,

142
00:04:47,168 --> 00:04:49,509
with a redesigned agent harness.

143
00:04:49,529 --> 00:04:50,810
Cohere told VentureBeat

144
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:53,405
that context, across sessions and agents,

145
00:04:53,425 --> 00:04:54,270
now persists longer,

146
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:56,055
so they don't start cold each time.

147
00:04:56,075 --> 00:04:57,676
Admins can set rate limits,

148
00:04:57,696 --> 00:04:59,460
user quotas, and company-wide caps,

149
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:02,187
and see how many tokens each agent uses.

150
00:05:02,207 --> 00:05:03,871
It deploys in the cloud, on premises,

151
00:05:03,891 --> 00:05:05,346
or fully air-gapped.

152
00:05:05,366 --> 00:05:06,286
Pricing isn't public.

153
00:05:06,306 --> 00:05:08,415
It's based on the scale and complexity of each deployment.

154
00:05:08,435 --> 00:05:10,808
When companies buy AI, the first question is often:

155
00:05:10,828 --> 00:05:12,387
will it blow the budget?

156
00:05:12,587 --> 00:05:13,327
Seventh,

157
00:05:13,347 --> 00:05:16,176
an open model built for hunting security bugs

158
00:05:16,196 --> 00:05:18,150
solved 40 of 60 tasks.

159
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:20,291
Security firm Cantina, with Yeta,

160
00:05:20,311 --> 00:05:23,791
released apex-flash-1 on October 1.

161
00:05:23,811 --> 00:05:26,341
It's built on Zhipu's GLM-5.3-Flash.

162
00:05:26,361 --> 00:05:29,034
How? It turned 50 real vulnerability cases

163
00:05:29,054 --> 00:05:30,626
into 150 training tasks

164
00:05:30,646 --> 00:05:32,976
and fine-tuned with reinforcement learning.

165
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:35,804
Cantina says that on 60 held-out internal tasks,

166
00:05:35,824 --> 00:05:37,326
it scored 66.7%,

167
00:05:37,346 --> 00:05:40,071
versus 60% for the base model.

168
00:05:40,091 --> 00:05:41,894
Claude Opus 5 High

169
00:05:41,914 --> 00:05:44,091
scored 71.7%, still higher.

170
00:05:44,111 --> 00:05:47,329
The weights are on Hugging Face under the MIT license.

171
00:05:47,349 --> 00:05:50,514
But it ran all 60 tasks for about $2.38,

172
00:05:50,534 --> 00:05:53,795
versus about $74.68 for Opus 5 High.

173
00:05:53,815 --> 00:05:56,604
Note: this is Cantina's own internal evaluation.

174
00:05:56,624 --> 00:05:58,014
Cheap models doing the work

175
00:05:58,034 --> 00:06:01,405
while pricey ones direct it may become more common.

176
00:06:01,605 --> 00:06:04,489
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

177
00:06:04,509 --> 00:06:07,334
Have a smooth day, and see you tomorrow.
